@using Odyssey.Client.Auth

@*
    OdsPasswordChangeForm — the shared current → new → confirm triad
    (Odyssey Design System · components/PasswordChangeForm).

    One component so that BOTH /account's "Change password" section AND the admin forced-reset gate
    (/change-password-required) render the identical fields, the same live OdsPasswordRules checklist,
    one submit button and one failure banner (issue #406). #405 extracted only the checklist, which was
    the right call while there was one copy of the triad; this issue would have created the second.

    Self-manages the three field values and raises OnSubmit only once the current field is filled, every
    PasswordPolicy rule is met, the two new entries match, and the new password differs from the current
    one. The host owns the outcome: pass Error for the failure banner and Busy while the request is in
    flight. Clear the fields after a success with Reset() (via @@ref) — the Blazor counterpart of the
    design system's "remount with a changed key".

    Accessibility, pinned here rather than re-implemented per surface:
      • every field declares its input purpose — the current one as a current password, the two new
        entries as new passwords (WCAG 1.3.5). A password manager's help matters MORE on the gate page,
        which the user reaches by an involuntary redirect, not less.
      • the failure banner is an OdsAlert with Severity.Error, so OdsAlert derives an assertive live
        role for it (WCAG 4.1.3). /account previously hand-rolled a banner that announced its ERROR
        variant politely, so moving both of its banners onto OdsAlert fixes that at the same time.

    (Both properties are pinned by source lints in PasswordSurfaceSourceTests, which count attribute
    occurrences in this file — so the notes above deliberately describe them in prose rather than
    quoting the markup, which would be counted as a second occurrence.)
*@

<form class="odc-pw-form @Class" @onsubmit="SubmitAsync" @onsubmit:preventDefault="true">
@* autocomplete and aria-describedby below are UNMATCHED attributes and have to stay that way.
   MudBlazor captures unmatched values into UserAttributes, and Blazor throws at render time if that
   property is ALSO assigned explicitly — "cannot be set explicitly when also used to capture unmatched
   values". Binding UserAttributes here (even to null) alongside autocomplete crashed both surfaces
   that render this form, so splatting a dictionary is not an option while any loose attribute remains.
   Keep this comment OUTSIDE the tag. A Razor comment placed among a component's attributes is not
   treated as a comment at all — it is parsed as an attribute name, and the browser then rejects it
   with "Failed to execute 'setAttribute'", truncating the rest of the form. *@
    <MudTextField @bind-Value="_current"
                  Label="Current password"
                  Placeholder="••••••••"
                  Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                  InputType="InputType.Password"
                  Immediate="true"
                  Required="true"
                  Disabled="Busy"
                  autocomplete="current-password"
                  aria-describedby="@DescribedBy"
                  AutoFocus="AutoFocus" />

    <div class="odc-pw-form-sep" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <MudTextField @bind-Value="_new"
                  Label="New password"
                  Placeholder="••••••••"
                  Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                  InputType="InputType.Password"
                  Immediate="true"
                  Required="true"
                  Disabled="Busy"
                  autocomplete="new-password"
                  Error="@SameAsCurrent"
                  ErrorText="Choose a password different from your current one." />

    <OdsPasswordRules Password="@_new" Columns="Columns" />

    <MudTextField @bind-Value="_confirm"
                  Label="Confirm new password"
                  Placeholder="••••••••"
                  Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                  InputType="InputType.Password"
                  Immediate="true"
                  Required="true"
                  Disabled="Busy"
                  autocomplete="new-password"
                  Error="@(_confirm.Length > 0 && !Matches)"
                  ErrorText="Passwords do not match."
                  HelperText="@(Matches && !SameAsCurrent ? "Passwords match." : null)" />

    @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Error))
    {
        <OdsAlert Severity="Severity.Error">@Error</OdsAlert>
    }

    <div class="odc-pw-form-actions">
        <MudButton Variant="Variant.Filled" Color="Color.Primary"
                   ButtonType="ButtonType.Submit"
                   StartIcon="@(Busy ? null : SubmitIcon)"
                   Disabled="@(!CanSubmit)">
            @if (Busy)
            {
                <OdsSpinner Class="me-2" Decorative="true" />
                @BusyLabel
            }
            else
            {
                @SubmitLabel
            }
        </MudButton>
    </div>
</form>

@code {
    private string _current = string.Empty;
    private string _new = string.Empty;
    private string _confirm = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>Raised with the two passwords once the form is valid and submitted. The host owns the request.</summary>
    [Parameter] public EventCallback<PasswordChange> OnSubmit { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Failure text for the banner — rendered as an <c>OdsAlert Severity.Error</c> (role="alert").</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Error { get; set; }

    /// <summary>Disables the fields and the submit, and swaps in the busy label, while a request is in flight.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Busy { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string SubmitLabel { get; set; } = "Update password";

    [Parameter] public string BusyLabel { get; set; } = "Updating…";

    [Parameter] public string SubmitIcon { get; set; } = Icons.Material.Filled.LockReset;

    /// <summary>Checklist layout — 1 (stacked, the narrow auth card) or 2 (grid, a wide form).</summary>
    [Parameter] public int Columns { get; set; } = 1;

    /// <summary>Focus the current-password field on mount.</summary>
    [Parameter] public bool AutoFocus { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Id of an element describing why the caller is being asked for a password — associated with the
    /// first field, so a screen-reader user who jumps straight into the form still hears it.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// <para>
    /// It has to land on a field. <c>aria-describedby</c> applies only to the element carrying it, so
    /// wrapping this component in a role-less <c>&lt;div&gt;</c> that holds the attribute associates it
    /// with nothing: the div isn't exposed to assistive tech, and the relationship does not cascade to
    /// descendants. WCAG 1.3.1.
    /// </para>
    /// <para>
    /// Rendered as a plain attribute on the first field rather than through a <c>UserAttributes</c>
    /// dictionary — see the comment there. Blazor omits an attribute whose value is null, so the
    /// unset case adds nothing to the markup.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter] public string? DescribedBy { get; set; }

    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    private bool Matches => _confirm.Length > 0 && _new == _confirm;

    // Surfaced as a field error rather than silently disabling the button, so the user is told why.
    // The server enforces it too — a no-op change would otherwise clear the forced-reset flag without
    // rotating anything, which is the whole point of the gate.
    private bool SameAsCurrent => _new.Length > 0 && _new == _current;

    // Driven by the same PasswordPolicy call that renders the checklist, so the button and the ticks
    // can never disagree.
    private bool CanSubmit =>
        _current.Length > 0 && PasswordPolicy.IsSatisfied(_new) && Matches && !SameAsCurrent && !Busy;

    private async Task SubmitAsync()
    {
        if (!CanSubmit)
        {
            return;
        }

        await OnSubmit.InvokeAsync(new PasswordChange(_current, _new));
    }

    /// <summary>Clears the three fields — call after a success so the form isn't left holding a live password.</summary>
    public void Reset()
    {
        _current = string.Empty;
        _new = string.Empty;
        _confirm = string.Empty;
        StateHasChanged();
    }

    /// <summary>The two passwords a submitted form carries.</summary>
    public sealed record PasswordChange(string CurrentPassword, string NewPassword);
}
